# EXAMPLE ONLY -- this file is NOT shipped in the image and is NOT read by
# anything. It is the shape of the file plus the reasoning behind it.
#
# Every address, MAC and router-id below has been replaced by a placeholder.
# Put your own in
#
#     /mnt/data/config/network.conf
#
# which lives on the data partition, so it survives an upgrade, a rollback and
# a reinstall -- and is never overwritten by an image built for this model.
#
# ⚠ THIS FILE USED TO CARRY ONE PHYSICAL SWITCH'S IDENTITY, IN A PUBLIC REPO.
# An image built from it made a second switch a duplicate of the first: same
# management address, same MAC, same OSPF router-id in the same area. Keep real
# values in <name>.site.conf, which .gitignore excludes, or on the switch.
#
# Network configuration for the Cisco Nexus 3172TQ.
#
# Addresses for one physical switch in one lab. Shipped rather than generated
# because none of it is the vendor's: it is how this unit is cabled and
# addressed, and somebody installing NOSaic on their own 3172TQ will replace
# every line.
#
# Syntax: iface <name> <addr/prefix> [mac <mac>] [mtu <n>]
#         route <prefix|default> via <gw> [dev <iface>]

# ── Management ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# ⚠ THE MAC IS "auto" BECAUSE THE NIC'S OWN EEPROM IS BLANK.
#
# eth0 comes up as 00:a0:c9:00:00:00 -- Intel's OUI with an all-zero suffix,
# which is an unprogrammed NIC EEPROM. That is true of the model and not of
# one unit, so EVERY 3172TQ presents that same address until something says
# otherwise.
#
# This line used to state <this switch's MAC>, which is correct for exactly one
# switch and wrong for every other -- an image built from it made the next
# unit present the first one's identity, which is worse than the blank
# default. "auto" asks the board instead: its ID PROM, the 24c512 at 0x52
# that the i2c service already instantiates, carries the allocated block at
# offset 4280 with a count of 0x0080, 128 addresses. idprom.go decodes it,
# Identity carries it, and apply-network.sh resolves "auto" through
# `nosaic platform mac`.
#
# .2 is NX-OS's address on this box and .3 is ours, deliberately kept apart so
# that whichever OS is running can be reached without disturbing the other's
# ARP state upstream. Both present the same MAC, which is fine -- one address
# per OS, one MAC for the board.
iface eth0 <address>/<prefix> mac auto
route default via <address>
# The build host, which is also the TFTP server every netboot comes from.
route <address>/<prefix> via <address> dev eth0

# ── Routing identity ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A stable address that does not belong to any one link, so a neighbour losing
# an interface does not lose the router. .50 and .53 are the Aristas, .241 is
# the Edgecore.
iface lo <address>/<prefix>

# ── Transit: the two copper ports patched to the Arista 7050TX-64 ──────────
#
# Panel 31 and 32 here to panel 31 and 32 there. That board is the same ASIC
# generation and the same BCM84848 copper PHY, and until this cable existed
# neither board had a routing neighbour -- so this pair is the first real
# traffic test for both of them.
#
# /29 per link, from the lab's <address>/<prefix> transit range. Blocks .40-.47,
# .48-.55, .56-.63, .64-.71, .72-.79 and .80-.87 are already spoken for by the
# other three switches; these two are not.
#
#   eth1_31  <address>/<prefix>   .33 here, .34 on the Arista
#   eth1_32  <address>/<prefix>   .97 here, .98 on the Arista
#
# ⚠ The two links are NOT in adjacent blocks, and that is the Arista's
# numbering rather than a mistake here. et31 sits in .<host>/<prefix> and et32 in
# .<host>/<prefix>; this end was originally written as .<host>/<prefix> and .<host>/<prefix>, which put the
# first link in a different subnet from its own far end -- a cable with link,
# addresses on both ends and no possible adjacency.
#
# ⚠ No mtu here, deliberately. The taps are created at 1500 by config/asic.conf
# and the other boards' transit links run 1600; raising these is a two-line
# change but BOTH ENDS must change together, or the link passes small frames
# and silently drops the rest.
iface eth1_31 <address>/<prefix>
iface eth1_32 <address>/<prefix>

# ── Transit: the 40G cage to the 7050SX2 ───────────────────────────────────
#
# Panel 54 here (logical port 69, the sixth QSFP cage) to et49 on the
# 7050SX2. This is the first 40G link this board has ever had and the first
# use of a cage at all: they are retimed through BCM84328s rather than
# direct-attach, which is the one part of this board's port map that has
# never been exercised. The cage reports 40000.
#
# ⚠ mtu 1600 to match that board, which runs all its transit at 1600. The
# tap is created at 1600 by config/asic.conf; this line states it again so
# the file says what the interface is, and so lowering it later means
# changing one place that is obviously about this link.
#
#   eth1_54  <address>/<prefix>   .90 here, .89 on the 7050SX2
#
# ⚠ .90 here, not .89. The plan in this file was the other way round, and the
# 7050SX2 was configured with .89 on its end before we booted with ours --
# measured on it, not assumed. Two ends claiming .89 is a duplicate address
# on a point-to-point link: it half-works, because each end answers its own
# ARP, and it presents as an adjacency that will not form for no visible
# reason.
iface eth1_54 <address>/<prefix> mtu 1600

# ── Transit: the second 40G cage, to the Edgecore AS5610 ──────────────────
#
# Panel 53 here (logical port 65, the fifth cage) to swp49 on the AS5610.
# That board already carries <address>/<prefix> on swp49 and runs it at 1600,
# both read off the running switch rather than chosen here, so this end
# takes .17 out of the same block and matches the MTU.
#
# This is the cage that proved the 40G path works: with a different module
# in it (AVF2146U0TX) it linked to the Edgecore at 40G immediately, while
# panel 54's module (AVF2146U1DH) has never linked to anything.
iface eth1_53 <address>/<prefix> mtu 1600
